    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96386.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Well, welcome to the Jason and the House podcast. I'm Jason Chaffetz. And thank you so much for giving us part of your day to have this interesting discussion. I'm really excited about this. We've got a former colleague of mine. His name is Jody Heis. He was Congressman in Georgia. And you know what? A super good guy. So I started in Congress a little over eight years and you interact with people. And you know what? It is a good cross-section of America. and i'm not saying based on party lines i'm just saying is you interact with with people you find people with all kinds of backgrounds and i think that's what our founders envisioned right they envisioned that a time uh... where america would be represented and it's hard because our population is is grown right average member average member of congress uh... represents about eight hundred thousand people in every ten years to go back look at the census rediviate up uh... and uh... and cuts well you meet people from all walks of life and we're going to have a conversation with uh... jody heists jody uh... he's just a wonderful wonderful human being and he wrote a book about his time in congress he recently left congress and uh... i think it'll be a good discussion about what is working what is not working i think a lot of people are looking at congress and saying uh... this is so dysfunctional uh... but they're things that are working in their things that really are working and so i want to have that discussion with uh... jody and uh... in the congressman heist and uh... and uh... get his perspective on that we're going to talk a little bit about the news and then we're gonna highlight the stupid because you know there's always somebody doing something stupid somewhere All right, I want to talk about the news here because news is volatile, right? There's always something that's going on with Trump or Biden. I think it's fascinating that with the new inflation numbers going back up that the president, Biden is going to try to argue, who knows what? I mean, I think he's going to say, oh, it's Donald Trump's fault. The Trump tax cuts this and that and he'll probably, you know, probably won't be saying that, you know, Bidenomics is working there's nothing that i think they can point to but there's a couple other factors of things going on in the economy that i don't hear a lot of the talking had analysis going through and i i want to discuss that first of all is our national debt uh... we have some thirty four trillion dollars in debt if you were to spend a million dollars a day everyday it would take you almost three thousand years to get the one trillion now we have thirty four trillion dollars in debt and the interest on that national debt uh... is eclipsing the size in the amount of money that we are spending on our national defense We are closing in and it'll take a little while to get there, unfortunately, but we will spend close to a trillion dollars a year just paying interest on the debt that's been accumulated. That is a stunningly high number that has to be dealt with. You know, every time we have a disaster, every time we have something happen, you know, we had this bridge collapse in in in Maryland. It was horrific. It was terrible. But have you heard anybody in Washington, DC having a discussion about, well, look, this disaster happened. You know, it's terrible. We got to fix it so we can continue on with commerce. So instead, we're going to have to dismantle or maybe not spend money on this so we can fix that. We never have that discussion. If that happened in a business or in a family, you'd make a tough decision and say, I'm sorry, we're not going to be able to do this because we got to do that. And then we never have a discussion about where we're going to cut money because we're always doing continuing resolutions and omnibuses. And there's always a promise, oh, we're going to cut a little bit. And then it never comes to fruition. Another factor, I think, on the economy and inflation is the fact that Americans post-covid had this insatiable desire to go out and do more things and there was this uh... uh... assurance by president biden and comala Harris that all inflationary is just transitory it's not really happening it'll come right down it's going down i think that p the people people a lot of false hope their own personal financial situation was going to rapidly improve um... and it hasn't and so consequently americans have more than one trillion dollars in credit card debt now statistically for listening to this this uh... podcast you probably have some of this credit card debt it's that pervasive and when you're paying twenty twenty two twenty four percent on interest that is a stunningly high number and it's not going to get better it's not as if interest rates are going to come down uh... and it's creating a financial burden on the economy that is just untenable the other thing that we've got a talk about is how big and uh... how pervasive this illegal immigration problems because with illegal immigration You can't port millions and millions of people into the economy and then not expect there to be an effect on housing and on the just demand for basic services. You have big cities and in a lot of these democratic run cities, big ones out there complaining that, oh my goodness, we need millions of dollars in order to take care of this. If you have nearly 10 million people here legally, they got to live somewhere. And then we're surprised when the cost of rent goes up across the country. Of course, that's going to happen. This is basic economics. But guess what? They're going to need more than rent and they're going to need more than a place to live. They're going to need food. They're going to need. to get educated, they're going to want to get a job, they're going to want to do all of these things, and they're going to have healthcare, they're going to have babies, they're going to do all kinds of things that are very expensive, and guess who's going to pay for that? We're going to pay for that. But it also, I think, has an effect on inflation, and that's one of the core problems. And so those are the types of things that I don't think you'll hear anybody talking about, but they really are going to be problematic. So, that's just one of the things that's happening in the news. I do want to get on to stupid because, you know what, there's always somebody doing something stupid somewhere. all right this is on foxnews.com new state department diversity chief believes the u.s. is a quote failed historical model with a colonizing past unbelievable but they bite administration's diversity chief These types of positions, he's driving that. But he said that America has a failed historical model and demanded the destruction of tradition, quote, at every junction. This is somebody who doesn't believe in America and is going to be in charge of diversity. So instead of getting a job based on merit, they're gonna be including people and hiring people based on their skin color. This is so totally polar opposite of what Martin Luther King was talking about. And the whole message, how they have embraced this with so much vigor is unbelievable. And I think it's just very offensive to a lot of people in this country, but to suggest that this Zakiah Carr Johnson, either chief diversity and inclusion officer, is just stupid. You're listening to Jason in the house. We'll be right back. Stay with us.",
