  {
    "episode_id": 96386,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96386.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, look, what the end goal is, let's start there, is one legal vote cast and one legal vote counted. Right. That's where we're trying to get to. So if you start at that end result and start going back from there, well, then you cannot know if you have a legal vote cast If you don't know that the one casting the vote is an eligible voter or to cast a vote. So we have to have clean as much as possible. And look, people move all the time, they die, they come and go, it's impossible to have 100% clean voter records, I suppose. And that's understandable. But as much as possible, the average state, I saw an article last week, the average state is between eight and 18% inaccurate in their voter rolls. their registration files. So if we are starting this game with anywhere from 8 to 18 percent inaccurate roles, that's problematic potentially. And so we have to do a better job. Why is it that Amazon knows where you are and who you are and where you live? But our federal government doesn't. I mean, from the post office to Amazon to the coroner's office. I mean, we can go down so many different places, know exactly who you are, where you are, where you move, when you move, it's all out there. It is not a matter that these vault roles are impossible to keep relatively accurate is the lack of commitment to do so.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The end goal is one legal vote cast and one legal vote counted.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "To determine whether a vote is legal, it must be confirmed that the voter is eligible.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
